PARAS = [
    "For most adults today, reading is a silent activity. We move our eyes across a page or a screen, and the words appear only inside our heads. Many of us simply suppose that people have always read this way. In fact, for long periods of history, reading usually meant speaking. Texts were shared by being read aloud to groups, and even a person reading alone would often murmur* the words. Silent reading became the normal habit for most readers only much later.",
    "Once silent reading spread, reading aloud came to be seen as something for small children or for those who were still learning. A child sounds out each word; an adult, we suppose, has moved {1|beyond} that stage. Yet more and more teachers and researchers suggest that this view may be too simple. Reading aloud, they argue, does several things that silent reading does less well.",
    "The first concerns memory. When we read a sentence silently, we see it. When we read it aloud, we also produce it with our mouths and hear it with our ears. {2|Because the words reach the brain by more than one route, they seem to leave a deeper trace than words that are only seen.} Many students already know this from experience: lines they have practiced aloud for a school play are often easier to recall than notes they have only looked at.",
    "A second benefit involves attention. Silent readers can skim*. Our eyes jump ahead, skip difficult words, and sometimes reach the bottom of a page without having taken in a single idea. Reading aloud makes this much harder. {3|Every word has to be dealt with}, and a sentence that is badly understood will usually sound wrong when spoken. For this reason, some writers read their own drafts aloud before sending them to anyone else, because the ear notices awkward phrases that the eye passes over.",
    "Reading aloud is also a social act. When a parent reads a story to a child, the two share not only the words but also the pauses, the funny voices, and the questions that interrupt the story. Children who are read to regularly often come to connect books with warmth and attention, and this connection may encourage them to keep reading as they grow older. In some care homes, volunteers read newspapers or short stories to older residents, and the sessions often turn into lively conversations about [[4]].",
    "None of this means that silent reading is inferior*. It is far faster, and for long texts it is the only practical choice. A student who tried to read every textbook aloud would never finish. The question is not which method is better in general, but which is more {5|suitable} for a particular purpose. For checking the headlines, silent reading is perfect. For a poem, a difficult paragraph, or a passage we want to remember, the voice can serve as a strong ally.",
    "Some language teachers have long known this. Learners who read short texts aloud, again and again, often begin to notice how words are linked together in natural speech, and their own speaking becomes smoother. {6|What began as an exercise for beginners can turn out to be useful at every level.}",
    "Perhaps the simplest lesson is that reading involves the whole body more than we usually admit. {7|The next time a sentence refuses to make sense, it may be worth closing the door and saying it out loud.} The answer may be found not only on the page but also in the sound of our own voice.",
]
